  "tutorial_example_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 4,
    "lesson_title": "From Impulse Decomposition to System Output",
    "lesson_core_move": "Convert a weighted shifted-impulse input decomposition into the corresponding LTI output by replacing each shifted impulse with the matching shifted, scaled impulse response and superposing the responses.",
    "section_count": 5,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 4,
        "lesson_title": "From Impulse Decomposition to System Output",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to From Impulse Decomposition to System Output",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "For an LTI system, the impulse response $h(t)$ is the output produced by the input $\\delta(t)$. Once you know $h(t)$, a shifted impulse input uses the same response shape with the same shift: $\\delta(t-t_k)$ produces $h(t-t_k)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "If the shifted impulse is multiplied by a weight $a_k$, homogeneity carries that same weight to the output response. The weight multiplies the whole shifted response; it does not change the time shift.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "a_k\\delta(t-t_k) \\longmapsto a_k h(t-t_k)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "For a finite impulse decomposition, apply this rule to each impulse component separately. For example, if $x(t)=2\\delta(t-1)-\\delta(t-3)$, the two component responses are $2h(t-1)$ and $-h(t-3)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Linearity then adds the component responses. So a finite sum of weighted shifted impulses becomes the matching finite sum of shifted, scaled copies of the impulse response.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=\\sum_k a_k\\delta(t-t_k) \\quad \\longmapsto \\quad y(t)=\\sum_k a_k h(t-t_k)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 4,
        "lesson_title": "From Impulse Decomposition to System Output",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Output from a Single Shifted Impulse",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "An LTI system has impulse response $h(t)$. Find the output when $x(t)=\\delta(t-2)$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is that the input is one shifted impulse with unit weight. Since the system is LTI, the response to $\\delta(t)$ is $h(t)$, and a delay in the impulse produces the same delay in the impulse response.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\delta(t-t_k) \\longmapsto h(t-t_k)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Here the input is $\\delta(t-2)$, so the shift is $t_k=2$. Replace the shifted impulse with the matching shifted impulse response.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=\\delta(t-2) \\quad \\Longrightarrow \\quad y(t)=h(t-2)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Therefore, the output is $y(t)=h(t-2)$. The impulse is replaced by $h$, and the shift stays the same.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 4,
        "lesson_title": "From Impulse Decomposition to System Output",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Output from a Scaled Shifted Impulse",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "An LTI system has impulse response $h(t)$. Find the output when $x(t)=3\\delta(t-1)$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is that the input is still one shifted impulse, but now it has a coefficient. In $3\\delta(t-1)$, the shift is $t_k=1$ and the weight is $3$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "a_k\\delta(t-t_k) \\longmapsto a_k h(t-t_k)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "First use time invariance on the shifted impulse: $\\delta(t-1)$ produces $h(t-1)$. Then use homogeneity to carry the factor $3$ to the output response.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=3\\delta(t-1) \\quad \\Longrightarrow \\quad y(t)=3h(t-1)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Therefore, the output is $y(t)=3h(t-1)$. The coefficient scales the whole shifted response; it does not change the time shift.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 4,
        "lesson_title": "From Impulse Decomposition to System Output",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Output from a Finite Sum of Weighted Shifted Impulses",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "An LTI system has impulse response $h(t)$. Find the output when $x(t)=2\\delta(t)-\\delta(t-3)+4\\delta(t-5)$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is that the input is a finite sum of weighted shifted impulses. Treat each impulse term separately, convert it to the matching shifted and scaled copy of $h(t)$, and then add the response terms.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "a_k\\delta(t-t_k) \\longmapsto a_k h(t-t_k)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Separate the input into three terms: $2\\delta(t)$, $-\\delta(t-3)$, and $4\\delta(t-5)$. The first term has shift $0$, the second has weight $-1$ and shift $3$, and the third has weight $4$ and shift $5$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\begin{aligned} 2\\delta(t) &\\longmapsto 2h(t) \\\\ -\\delta(t-3) &\\longmapsto -h(t-3) \\\\ 4\\delta(t-5) &\\longmapsto 4h(t-5) \\end{aligned}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Because the system is linear, the output to the full input is the sum of these component responses.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t)=2h(t)-h(t-3)+4h(t-5)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the output is $y(t)=2h(t)-h(t-3)+4h(t-5)$. Each impulse was replaced by $h$ with the same shift, and each coefficient stayed attached to its own response term.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 4,
        "lesson_title": "From Impulse Decomposition to System Output",
        "section_index": 5,
        "section_title": "Superposing Responses from a Weighted Impulse Decomposition",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "An LTI system has impulse response $h(t)$, and $x(t)=\\sum_{k=1}^{3}a_k\\delta(t-t_k)$ with $a_1=2$, $t_1=0$, $a_2=-1$, $t_2=2$, and $a_3=3$, $t_3=5$. Write $y(t)$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is that the input is given as a compact indexed impulse decomposition. Read each coefficient $a_k$ and shift $t_k$, then make one output term $a_kh(t-t_k)$ for each index.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=\\sum_{k=1}^{3}a_k\\delta(t-t_k) \\quad \\Longrightarrow \\quad y(t)=\\sum_{k=1}^{3}a_k h(t-t_k)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Now substitute the listed $a_k$ and $t_k$ values into the output terms. The index $k$ only labels each component; the coefficient comes from $a_k$, and the shift comes from $t_k$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\begin{aligned} k=1:\\quad a_1h(t-t_1) &= 2h(t-0)=2h(t) \\\\ k=2:\\quad a_2h(t-t_2) &= (-1)h(t-2)=-h(t-2) \\\\ k=3:\\quad a_3h(t-t_3) &= 3h(t-5) \\end{aligned}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Superpose the three component responses to form the full output.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t)=2h(t)-h(t-2)+3h(t-5)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Therefore, $y(t)=2h(t)-h(t-2)+3h(t-5)$. This is the same finite-sum rule as before; the only difference is that the weights and shifts were provided in indexed form.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  },
